WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:04.250
Airships have always
gotten people's attention.

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:05.670
Airships are magical.

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:07.290
They're fascinating.

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:09.700
Whenever a film director or producer wants

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:12.200
to telegraph this is an alternate reality,

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:14.550
you'll see an airship in the sky.

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:16.680
We see that already.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.930
There are several airship projects

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:21.280
that are already been initiated,

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:26.280
so it seems that the airship
industry has been reborn.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:30.200
Nearly a century after
falling out of favor,

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.510
airships could be on the rise again,

00:00:32.510 --> 00:00:34.740
this time as a potential
solution to one of

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:39.410
the most elusive problems in
aerospace, reducing emissions.

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:42.280
The tremendous amount of the
fuel burned by an airliner

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:45.120
is burned simply to stay in the air,

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:46.790
and an airship doesn't need to do that.

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:49.060
Backed by new research and technology,

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:52.140
companies are aiming to take
advantage of this free lift

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:56.140
to fill in some gaps underserved
by current infrastructure.

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:58.773
And of course, there's
always the excitement factor.

00:00:59.668 --> 00:01:01.100
It's something that is very attractive

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:02.610
to people as an idea.

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:04.280
It's a type of aircraft that seems

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:07.270
to generate an emotional response

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:09.930
But for airships to go
from alternative reality

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:13.870
to a reality, they'll need
to support a business case

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:16.296
that can overcome nearly
a century of failure.

00:01:16.296 --> 00:01:17.830
Oh, the humanity!

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:20.620
If you look at it, every
project ever failed

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:21.720
regarding large capacity airship.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:24.470
You don't have large airship in the sky.

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:26.610
It's interesting that we
don't look back on things

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:28.740
like the Titanic as the end of shipping,

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:30.950
because shipping has been
an economically viable

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:34.380
and mainstream part of our
transport networks ever since.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:37.280
The first thing to reshape that
narrative on the technology

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:40.313
is to make it an economically
viable proposition.

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:51.790
A century ago, airships, dirigibles,

00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:56.000
and Zeppelins were at the
cutting edge of luxury transport.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:59.230
Ferrying passengers non-stop
across the Atlantic Ocean,

00:01:59.230 --> 00:02:01.640
decades before the jet age.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:04.080
Today, the number of active
airships has dwindled

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:06.070
to a few dozen worldwide,

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:09.853
mostly used for novelty rides
or as floating billboards.

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:12.520
But a handful of brave companies

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:14.190
around the world are trying to make them

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:16.473
into a more common sight in the skies.

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:20.170
Some like LTA Research funded

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:22.610
by Google co-founder Sergey Brin,

00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:25.350
have been rather secretive
about their plans.

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:28.050
Other companies have only
released computer-generated

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:30.300
visions of what they hope to build.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:34.070
A few, however, have been able
to actually fly a prototype.

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:37.530
It is the largest aircraft in the world.

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:40.100
Makers boasted that they had created

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:43.280
a new breed of hyper-efficient aircraft.

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:45.110
This is the Airlander 10,

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:48.170
endearingly nicknamed
"The Flying Buttocks".

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:50.640
It makes up for what it lacks in style

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:53.130
by offering what could be
one of the greenest ways

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:54.730
to travel by air.

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:57.570
Using around 1.2 million cubic feet

00:02:57.570 --> 00:02:59.460
of lighter-than-air helium,

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:01.440
the craft can cruise at a top speed

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:03.420
of about 80 miles per hour.

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:05.630
Originally designed as
a surveillance platform

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:06.880
by the U.S. military,

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:09.110
the project was canceled in 2013,

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:12.800
but UK-based manufacturer
Hybrid Air Vehicles

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:14.600
would eventually re-acquire the project

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:15.500
to bring it to market.

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:17.903
So, it's a carbon-fiber construction.

00:03:17.903 --> 00:03:20.080
And as you can see, as you come in here,

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:23.840
into the flight deck element,
there's loads of space here.

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:28.173
If you are the pilot,
your view out is amazing.

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:31.490
From an aircraft-development perspective,

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:33.360
you can do a huge amount on paper.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:36.120
You can do a huge amount in
simulation and wind tunnels,

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:38.380
but it's getting a new
configuration into flight.

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:40.933
That's the big milestone
because you learn a lot.

00:03:41.770 --> 00:03:44.570
Its second test flight left
some onlookers feeling,

00:03:44.570 --> 00:03:46.770
let's just say, deflated.

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:48.620
Oh my God, he's actually just broken it.

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:50.850
Thankfully, far from the
worst airship disaster

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:53.510
of all time, the Airlander
eventually returned

00:03:53.510 --> 00:03:55.380
to the skies for more testing.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:59.040
Since 2017, this Airlander
10 prototype resides mostly

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:01.200
in pieces at HAV's headquarters,

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.590
just over an hour outside London.

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:07.570
As troubling as it looks,
falling, ideally or gracefully,

00:04:07.570 --> 00:04:10.460
is actually a key feature
of how the craft flies.

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:13.230
As the company name Hybrid
Air Vehicle suggests,

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:15.980
Airlander is what's
known as a hybrid airship

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:18.070
that is achieving lift from a combination

00:04:18.070 --> 00:04:21.730
of conventional airship design
and aerodynamic attributes

00:04:21.730 --> 00:04:23.400
from other flying aircraft.

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:25.460
The principal element at play is helium,

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:28.430
the lighter-than-air gas
that has the added perk

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:30.270
of being non-flammable.

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:33.510
Unlike the hydrogen that
doomed the infamous Hindenburg.

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:35.670
Just like a toy balloon,
with enough helium,

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:39.120
the ship will rise, essentially free lift.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:42.283
The trouble with free
lift is it's always there.

00:04:43.150 --> 00:04:44.810
And so we had to find a way

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:48.230
of making the aircraft work more easily

00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:51.050
in terms of how things were
loaded onto it and off it.

00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:52.610
And they made the operation

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:55.600
for the the whoever's flying it easier.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:57.430
We've done that by making the aircraft

00:04:57.430 --> 00:04:59.380
generate aerodynamic lift.

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:04.130
So it takes off and lands
like a conventional aircraft,

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:07.070
although in a really short space.

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:10.510
That lift is effectively
only lifting the payload.

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:12.730
That's the easiest way to think of it.

00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:15.120
This means that without
the aerodynamic lift,

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:17.090
the craft is actually heavier than air,

00:05:17.090 --> 00:05:18.360
more like an airplane.

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:20.870
Without the lift generated
by forward motion,

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:23.490
the airship sinks down
and more importantly,

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:24.760
stays on the ground.

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:26.290
And the reason that's important

00:05:26.290 --> 00:05:28.900
is because getting on board
and offboard an airplane,

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:30.730
taking a shipping container
on board or offboard

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:33.532
an airplane, it's a lot easier
if you're on the ground.

00:05:36.313 --> 00:05:39.190
And some of the large
airships would use, you know,

00:05:39.190 --> 00:05:43.150
ground crews of up to
500 people to get them

00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:44.870
out of hangers and move them around.

00:05:44.870 --> 00:05:46.710
Even the smaller airships of today

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:48.950
have got significant ground crew.

00:05:48.950 --> 00:05:50.120
With a hybrid aircraft,

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:52.490
we're on the ground for that
loading and unloading cycle.

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:54.560
It allows us to turn the
aircraft around quickly

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:56.040
and drive the economics,

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:58.400
the economic performance of
the aircraft as an asset.

00:05:58.400 --> 00:05:59.760
This means the craft can take off

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:02.090
and land from any reasonably flat surface

00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:05.170
like grass, water, or even ice.

00:06:05.170 --> 00:06:06.810
And according to HAV,

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:09.530
the base model that uses
four combustion engines

00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:14.290
will reduce CO2 emissions by
75% over comparable aircraft.

00:06:14.290 --> 00:06:16.640
And future plans to have
a hybrid electric engine

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:19.950
would reduce emissions
by 90% with the goal

00:06:19.950 --> 00:06:23.513
to eventually create a
truly emissions-free craft.

00:06:23.513 --> 00:06:26.470
HAV believes that this
combination of technologies

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:28.930
will make Airlander both eco-conscious

00:06:28.930 --> 00:06:31.260
and economically viable for applications

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:34.850
ranging from surveillance,
logistics, cargo transport,

00:06:34.850 --> 00:06:36.580
and even passenger travel.

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:38.590
When I think about passenger transport,

00:06:38.590 --> 00:06:43.230
it's such an exciting
space to move a technology

00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:46.283
like this into because it
can change our expectations.

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:53.360
Right now, if we want to make
a journey here in the UK,

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:57.870
say, from the Northwest
of the UK to Ireland,

00:06:57.870 --> 00:06:59.470
we've got a body of water to cross.

00:06:59.470 --> 00:07:02.130
And our choices are to
travel out to an airport,

00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:04.070
do it very fast, but very short flight

00:07:04.070 --> 00:07:05.460
and then travel back in from the airport.

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:06.700
at the other end.

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:09.870
Or we can get on a
ferry that's three times

00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:11.150
the journey length.

00:07:11.150 --> 00:07:12.450
And the right thing to do here

00:07:12.450 --> 00:07:14.860
from a carbon-footprint
perspective is to get on the ferry.

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:18.000
But it's such a time-impact for people.

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:20.040
With Airlander, what we offer is almost

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:21.763
a high-speed train of the sky.

00:07:23.470 --> 00:07:27.180
Added to that, the aircraft
will go point to point.

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:29.990
It's not constrained to
airports that we have today.

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:31.910
So like a helicopter, it'll take off

00:07:31.910 --> 00:07:33.480
and land away from the airport.

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:35.956
So we can open up a whole new journey.

00:07:35.956 --> 00:07:40.003
I mean, an obvious example
is going to the North Pole.

00:07:40.003 --> 00:07:42.270
HAV advertises that Airlander

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:43.630
will be able to carry passengers

00:07:43.630 --> 00:07:47.010
to remote areas like the North
Pole on three-day excursions,

00:07:47.010 --> 00:07:50.440
covering up to 2,000 nautical
miles at cruising speeds

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:52.670
of around 60 miles per hour.

00:07:52.670 --> 00:07:54.200
A bit like with a helicopter,

00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:57.650
you can similarly take
helicopters to almost anywhere,

00:07:57.650 --> 00:08:00.200
but the range of a
helicopter's very short,

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:03.090
whereas, you know, we can
go on a three-day cruise

00:08:03.090 --> 00:08:05.760
if you like, with a
stop at the North Pole.

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:07.110
Their first commercial target

00:08:07.110 --> 00:08:09.750
is to bring this experience
to the luxury travel market.

00:08:09.750 --> 00:08:14.750
So I'm sat here in our leisure
cabin demonstrator here

00:08:14.830 --> 00:08:17.060
with the bar, with social spaces.

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:18.690
And what you can't see is down

00:08:18.690 --> 00:08:20.370
at the other end of this
cabin from where I'm sat,

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:25.070
there are eight double-bedrooms
for overnight cruises.

00:08:25.070 --> 00:08:27.820
This allows the leisure sector

00:08:27.820 --> 00:08:29.763
to offer their clients
experiences that you

00:08:29.763 --> 00:08:31.640
just can't get anywhere else.

00:08:31.640 --> 00:08:32.900
Their approach hearkens back

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:34.550
to the early 20th century,

00:08:34.550 --> 00:08:37.160
when luxurious airships
were the premium option

00:08:37.160 --> 00:08:41.270
for well-healed passengers
on trans-Atlantic journeys.

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:44.230
But building a sophisticated-looking
bar and filling it

00:08:44.230 --> 00:08:46.720
with an impressive wine selection is still

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:49.163
a far away from a happy hour in the skies.

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:52.070
Luxury travelers who
are going to be paying

00:08:52.070 --> 00:08:55.010
a tremendous amount of money
are probably gonna compare

00:08:55.010 --> 00:08:56.520
this maybe to a cruise ship.

00:08:56.520 --> 00:08:58.150
They're used to having a private bathroom

00:08:58.150 --> 00:08:59.250
with their bedroom.

00:08:59.250 --> 00:09:01.310
They're used to doing
things like taking showers,

00:09:01.310 --> 00:09:04.730
and you know, using water in the sink.

00:09:04.730 --> 00:09:07.270
You need to cater to their needs

00:09:07.270 --> 00:09:10.120
with a relatively high
staff-to-passenger ratio

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:12.570
if you're gonna deliver a luxury product.

00:09:12.570 --> 00:09:15.880
And everything I just mentioned is heavy.

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:17.890
A cruise ship on the
ocean doesn't really need

00:09:17.890 --> 00:09:21.620
to worry so much about
the weight of things.

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:23.150
You always have to look
at the alternative.

00:09:23.150 --> 00:09:26.170
We're gonna use an airship
or we're gonna use X, right?

00:09:26.170 --> 00:09:28.640
So let's say X is a high speed rail line.

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:30.850
So if you look at a place like Europe,

00:09:30.850 --> 00:09:35.600
high-speed trains can connect
city center to city center.

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:39.100
And high-speed trains go a lot
faster than any airship can.

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:40.560
They carry a lot of people.

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:43.820
They're very environmentally friendly.

00:09:43.820 --> 00:09:46.490
Are airship's beautiful,
and magical, and wonderful?

00:09:46.490 --> 00:09:48.050
Of course they are, right?

00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:51.010
But is a high-speed train
line make more sense

00:09:51.010 --> 00:09:53.240
to go from Berlin to Hamburg?

00:09:53.240 --> 00:09:54.480
Yeah, it probably does.

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:56.709
And in fact it almost certainly does.

00:09:56.709 --> 00:09:59.620
HAV though, believes
Airlander can serve a gap

00:09:59.620 --> 00:10:02.020
in areas where such
infrastructure is lacking

00:10:02.020 --> 00:10:03.550
at a much lower cost.

00:10:03.550 --> 00:10:05.910
Yeah, we've got hugely invested,

00:10:05.910 --> 00:10:08.890
high-speed rail infrastructure in Europe.

00:10:08.890 --> 00:10:10.350
There's lots of parts of the world where

00:10:10.350 --> 00:10:12.550
a lot of movements are over water.

00:10:12.550 --> 00:10:13.710
There's a lot of parts of the world

00:10:13.710 --> 00:10:15.410
where we've got big geographic boundaries,

00:10:15.410 --> 00:10:17.480
mountain ranges, and so forth.

00:10:17.480 --> 00:10:20.080
And so, in both of those cases, you know,

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:23.580
the ability to put ground
surface infrastructure

00:10:23.580 --> 00:10:24.480
is constrained.

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:26.340
You know, big infrastructure
programs needed

00:10:26.340 --> 00:10:27.630
if you're gonna open up high-speed rail.

00:10:27.630 --> 00:10:29.390
Big infrastructure programs
if you're gonna tunnel

00:10:29.390 --> 00:10:31.320
under the sea, rather than fly over it,

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:32.570
where Airlander can step in

00:10:32.570 --> 00:10:35.700
with a very infrastructure-light
solution and an experience.

00:10:35.700 --> 00:10:37.860
And a travel experience is very equivalent

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:39.830
to a high-speed rail of the skies.

00:10:39.830 --> 00:10:41.400
Beyond passenger travel,

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:43.900
the prospect of low-emission
transport without the need

00:10:43.900 --> 00:10:47.510
for major infrastructure, could
also have a profound impact

00:10:47.510 --> 00:10:49.563
in another space, cargo.

00:10:53.820 --> 00:10:58.240
This is the LTA60T, an
airship with a cargo capacity

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:01.020
of up to 60 tons, making it even larger

00:11:01.020 --> 00:11:03.640
than HAV's Airlander 10.

00:11:03.640 --> 00:11:05.340
It's the dream of Paris-based company

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:07.890
aptly named "Flying Whales".

00:11:07.890 --> 00:11:10.170
The reason why we created "Flying Whales"

00:11:10.170 --> 00:11:13.540
is actually to solve a major
issue we have in France.

00:11:13.540 --> 00:11:15.790
Forest industry was the second contributor

00:11:15.790 --> 00:11:17.950
to the France trade balance deficit

00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:20.140
because we have a lot of resource,

00:11:20.140 --> 00:11:23.950
but there is no return
investment to build road

00:11:23.950 --> 00:11:28.230
and tracks to pick up the logs
and bring it to the saw mill.

00:11:28.230 --> 00:11:32.760
So we had to invent a
solution to go by the air

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:34.657
With funding from the French government,

00:11:34.657 --> 00:11:37.200
"Flying Whales" began
to develop an airship

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:39.680
that could fly into the
remote mountains of France

00:11:39.680 --> 00:11:42.840
to retrieve logs for
processing into lumber.

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:44.070
As you might guess from only

00:11:44.070 --> 00:11:46.200
seeing computer-generated renderings,

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:49.010
the company has yet to
build a full prototype.

00:11:49.010 --> 00:11:51.110
But they believe that
after nearly a decade,

00:11:51.110 --> 00:11:52.210
they're getting close.

00:11:52.210 --> 00:11:54.960
So we've been through,
something like four years

00:11:54.960 --> 00:11:59.960
of what I call "de-risking
phase" on technology, markets.

00:12:00.350 --> 00:12:03.357
It's actually, ultimately, it's
half-a-billion Euro project.

00:12:03.357 --> 00:12:04.840
And you, you have to be sure

00:12:04.840 --> 00:12:06.960
about what you do when
you invest so much money.

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:11.960
And in 2017, we were
pretty much all convinced

00:12:12.190 --> 00:12:15.463
and then we actually launched
the engineering project.

00:12:16.730 --> 00:12:19.020
So here is the global view.

00:12:19.020 --> 00:12:22.820
You can see the external
shape details here,

00:12:22.820 --> 00:12:25.780
the stations for the propellers.

00:12:25.780 --> 00:12:27.780
On the left side, on the right side,

00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:30.890
and also the stations
for the zed propellers.

00:12:30.890 --> 00:12:32.710
We are very huge, 200 meters

00:12:32.710 --> 00:12:35.390
in length and 50 meters in height.

00:12:35.390 --> 00:12:37.500
And we have a rigid body

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:41.470
which means we don't
change shape according

00:12:41.470 --> 00:12:43.640
to the environmental conditions.

00:12:43.640 --> 00:12:46.440
This model of the LTA60T is designed

00:12:46.440 --> 00:12:49.770
to carry wood logs, but the
company believes it can also

00:12:49.770 --> 00:12:52.950
be used to efficiently
carry other cumbersome cargo

00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:57.380
like high-voltage towers
or wind turbine blades.

00:12:57.380 --> 00:13:00.900
Transporting large bulky items
on today's infrastructure,

00:13:00.900 --> 00:13:03.096
let's just say, can run
into some challenges.

00:13:03.096 --> 00:13:04.203
Oh, my God!

00:13:08.490 --> 00:13:10.930
And for remote areas like Northern Canada,

00:13:10.930 --> 00:13:14.040
it could open up entirely
new industries from resources

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:17.420
in regions lacking traditional
land or air infrastructure.

00:13:17.420 --> 00:13:20.460
But with airships, loading
and unloading cargo

00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:22.660
has a big technical caveat.

00:13:22.660 --> 00:13:25.600
The load-exchange problem
in an airship carrying cargo

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:27.170
is that when you release the cargo

00:13:27.170 --> 00:13:28.810
and you release its weight,

00:13:28.810 --> 00:13:31.410
what's gonna stop the
airship from just popping up

00:13:31.410 --> 00:13:34.390
into the sky because
it's now a lot lighter?

00:13:34.390 --> 00:13:37.100
So you gotta exchange that load somehow.

00:13:37.100 --> 00:13:38.960
There are a few different
ways to overcome this.

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:40.460
The most obvious solution might be

00:13:40.460 --> 00:13:43.650
to dynamically adjust the quantity of gas.

00:13:43.650 --> 00:13:46.510
But helium while much safer than hydrogen,

00:13:46.510 --> 00:13:49.420
is also much more expensive to acquire.

00:13:49.420 --> 00:13:52.940
So adding and releasing large
quantities during each flight,

00:13:52.940 --> 00:13:54.403
isn't economically feasible.

00:13:55.410 --> 00:13:58.030
But there may be another
physics-based solution here.

00:13:58.030 --> 00:14:01.100
By compressing or
decompressing the helium,

00:14:01.100 --> 00:14:03.920
you could change the airship's
lift while in flight.

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:05.977
This is ultimately the best solution.

00:14:05.977 --> 00:14:09.513
And some of our competitors
are trying to develop this.

00:14:10.350 --> 00:14:12.140
The problem we have with this solution

00:14:12.140 --> 00:14:13.410
that it's very heavy.

00:14:13.410 --> 00:14:15.660
Compressor and tanker are very heavy,

00:14:15.660 --> 00:14:18.080
so it needs a lot of R and D.

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:20.820
And I think this technology
is gonna be available

00:14:20.820 --> 00:14:23.030
in something like 5 to 10 years.

00:14:23.030 --> 00:14:25.390
Until then, another option
is off-setting weight

00:14:25.390 --> 00:14:27.170
with engine thrust,

00:14:27.170 --> 00:14:29.520
but this would largely defeat
the efficiency benefits

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:31.920
of an airship in the first place.

00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:33.550
Instead "Flying Whales" is considering

00:14:33.550 --> 00:14:35.750
a more balanced solution.

00:14:35.750 --> 00:14:37.490
If a trip calls for transporting say,

00:14:37.490 --> 00:14:39.580
30 tons of wind turbine blades,

00:14:39.580 --> 00:14:41.560
it would need to pick up another 30 tons

00:14:41.560 --> 00:14:43.700
to replace the weight after drop off

00:14:43.700 --> 00:14:45.860
with the help of thrusters
to help compensate

00:14:45.860 --> 00:14:47.860
during the transfer process.

00:14:47.860 --> 00:14:50.770
In the example of retrieving
wood from remote regions,

00:14:50.770 --> 00:14:52.500
it would need to carry
the equivalent weight

00:14:52.500 --> 00:14:54.470
in water on the trip out.

00:14:54.470 --> 00:14:57.800
Then dispense that water
dynamically as it lifts wood

00:14:57.800 --> 00:14:59.860
in order to achieve balance,

00:14:59.860 --> 00:15:03.623
which visually at least, kind
of looks like a flying whale.

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:09.480
In theory, it can be simple,
but impractical, it is not.

00:15:09.480 --> 00:15:11.273
Because it's a dynamic period.

00:15:12.590 --> 00:15:16.080
And during this dynamic period,
you have to be very careful

00:15:16.080 --> 00:15:18.753
on the equilibrium between
buoyancy and weight.

00:15:19.760 --> 00:15:22.360
As if this didn't sound
complicated enough,

00:15:22.360 --> 00:15:25.920
airships face another major
vulnerability, weather.

00:15:25.920 --> 00:15:27.720
The side of an airship

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:29.280
is like a giant wall.

00:15:29.280 --> 00:15:30.960
It's like a giant sail.

00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:32.890
And there is something
we call "Sail Effect".

00:15:32.890 --> 00:15:35.340
If you have a really, really big airship,

00:15:35.340 --> 00:15:39.460
even a gentle wind multiplied
by the huge expanse

00:15:39.460 --> 00:15:41.090
of the side of an airship,

00:15:41.090 --> 00:15:42.960
can really move that airship in a way

00:15:42.960 --> 00:15:46.900
that it can't move a smaller
vehicle, like a small airplane.

00:15:46.900 --> 00:15:48.010
To compensate for wind,

00:15:48.010 --> 00:15:51.143
the LTA60T has a series
of propellers positioned

00:15:51.143 --> 00:15:52.860
all the around the aircraft.

00:15:52.860 --> 00:15:55.590
These axes help us to control the airship

00:15:55.590 --> 00:15:58.840
in a longitudinal, vertical,
and a directional way.

00:15:58.840 --> 00:16:00.380
This ultimately limits application

00:16:00.380 --> 00:16:02.700
to areas with stable-enough
weather conditions

00:16:02.700 --> 00:16:05.360
where this weight
exchange can safely occur.

00:16:05.360 --> 00:16:08.000
Despite all of these
obstacles, "Flying Whales"

00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:11.090
still envisions viable
application for airships.

00:16:11.090 --> 00:16:14.580
Transport business is very tough.

00:16:14.580 --> 00:16:17.960
There is a lot of competition,
so margins are very small.

00:16:17.960 --> 00:16:21.337
So it's useless to try to
come with a new solution, say,

00:16:21.337 --> 00:16:23.580
"Okay, look, guy we're
gonna replace what you do

00:16:23.580 --> 00:16:26.237
because we have a green
solution or a lock solution."

00:16:26.237 --> 00:16:27.600
No, that doesn't work.

00:16:27.600 --> 00:16:29.850
When the system is already existing

00:16:29.850 --> 00:16:31.460
in the transport solution with low margin,

00:16:31.460 --> 00:16:32.710
you cannot replace it.

00:16:32.710 --> 00:16:35.840
So we just look at where we really provide

00:16:35.840 --> 00:16:39.070
a huge gap in terms of cost or capacity

00:16:39.070 --> 00:16:41.410
regarding the existing.

00:16:41.410 --> 00:16:42.990
And in addition, of course,

00:16:42.990 --> 00:16:45.630
we will strongly reduce
the environmental impact.

00:16:45.630 --> 00:16:46.790
The niche that "Flying Whales"

00:16:46.790 --> 00:16:48.670
is targeting is currently served

00:16:48.670 --> 00:16:51.713
by a more common site
in the sky, helicopters.

00:16:52.710 --> 00:16:54.267
Based on benchmark tests,

00:16:54.267 --> 00:16:55.950
"Flying Whales" claims that their airship

00:16:55.950 --> 00:16:58.240
could save 15 times the carbon emissions

00:16:58.240 --> 00:17:00.280
and carry three times more payload

00:17:00.280 --> 00:17:02.800
than the best performing
heavy-lift helicopters,

00:17:02.800 --> 00:17:05.260
while still being cheaper to produce.

00:17:05.260 --> 00:17:09.860
The helicopter has been
developed to transport

00:17:09.860 --> 00:17:14.250
very, very fast people
from point A to point B.

00:17:14.250 --> 00:17:17.810
I mean, planes have been
developed to go very fast,

00:17:17.810 --> 00:17:20.270
very, very far.

00:17:20.270 --> 00:17:23.283
Okay. In our case, we are
something like a flying crane.

00:17:24.460 --> 00:17:27.990
We are today, the designer of the airship,

00:17:27.990 --> 00:17:31.580
but tomorrow we'll be the
producer and the operator.

00:17:31.580 --> 00:17:33.650
So, in the future, our business model

00:17:33.650 --> 00:17:35.730
will be based on two things.

00:17:35.730 --> 00:17:38.490
The production and direct
sale of the airship

00:17:38.490 --> 00:17:40.630
by Flying Whales Industry.

00:17:40.630 --> 00:17:44.100
And the transportation services provided

00:17:44.100 --> 00:17:45.823
by Flying Whales Services.

00:17:46.807 --> 00:17:48.330
"Flying Whales" hopes to be flying

00:17:48.330 --> 00:17:51.070
their airship by late 2024.

00:17:51.070 --> 00:17:53.170
In the meantime, they
will test and finalize

00:17:53.170 --> 00:17:56.020
their design while working
with aviation regulators

00:17:56.020 --> 00:17:58.480
to approve the designs for flight.

00:17:58.480 --> 00:18:00.480
You know, building any new aircraft,

00:18:00.480 --> 00:18:03.580
getting any brand new
aircraft design certificated

00:18:03.580 --> 00:18:06.290
by the, you know,
certificating authorities

00:18:07.150 --> 00:18:09.223
is a very, very expensive project.

00:18:10.200 --> 00:18:11.570
Now, when you're building

00:18:11.570 --> 00:18:14.370
on a previous iteration
of something, you know,

00:18:14.370 --> 00:18:19.370
you're turning a 757 into
a 767 or a 727 into a 737,

00:18:21.980 --> 00:18:23.580
you're still certificating new aircraft.

00:18:23.580 --> 00:18:25.180
It's still a lot of engineering.

00:18:25.180 --> 00:18:28.800
It's still a lot of expense,

00:18:28.800 --> 00:18:30.510
but you're starting with something.

00:18:30.510 --> 00:18:34.350
Building an airship in
the 2020s is sort of

00:18:34.350 --> 00:18:36.500
starting with a blank
slate because nobody's

00:18:36.500 --> 00:18:39.530
had a giant airship since the 1930s.

00:18:39.530 --> 00:18:41.430
But what if money isn't everything?

00:18:41.430 --> 00:18:43.410
At least in the beginning?

00:18:43.410 --> 00:18:45.680
Achieving our goals in
preserving the planet

00:18:45.680 --> 00:18:47.530
might take a mix of ingenuity

00:18:47.530 --> 00:18:50.420
and adopting unconventional approaches.

00:18:50.420 --> 00:18:52.360
And that begins with niche applications

00:18:52.360 --> 00:18:56.220
of new, unproven, and
even unprofitable tech,

00:18:56.220 --> 00:18:57.653
for the time being at least.

00:18:58.800 --> 00:19:00.980
The two challenge you have is first

00:19:00.980 --> 00:19:02.890
to prepare your shareholders.

00:19:02.890 --> 00:19:04.103
It's gonna be very long.

00:19:04.103 --> 00:19:06.240
It's gonna cost a lot.

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:08.170
Okay. And the second thing,

00:19:08.170 --> 00:19:10.670
which is actually one
of my major challenge

00:19:10.670 --> 00:19:14.080
is to keep your team motivated.

00:19:14.080 --> 00:19:16.800
When you have a young
engineer coming in the company

00:19:16.800 --> 00:19:21.110
and after two years, three
years, five years, six years,

00:19:21.110 --> 00:19:22.147
you have to tell them,

00:19:22.147 --> 00:19:23.770
"Well, you still have four years

00:19:23.770 --> 00:19:25.990
before you're going to see that flying".

00:19:25.990 --> 00:19:27.430
It is very difficult.

00:19:27.430 --> 00:19:30.380
We are seeing a lot of
companies talk about airships.

00:19:30.380 --> 00:19:33.600
We're not seeing a lot of
companies building airships.

00:19:33.600 --> 00:19:34.830
There are things that an airship

00:19:34.830 --> 00:19:35.940
can do better than anything else.

00:19:35.940 --> 00:19:37.870
It's just that there are not very many

00:19:37.870 --> 00:19:39.470
of those things, right?

00:19:39.470 --> 00:19:41.470
It's always gonna be a niche player.

00:19:41.470 --> 00:19:43.510
If it's gonna be a player at all,

00:19:43.510 --> 00:19:46.300
it's going to fill a
very, very narrow niche.

00:19:46.300 --> 00:19:48.720
Coming into this business
and leading this business,

00:19:48.720 --> 00:19:50.910
it's been really, really important

00:19:50.910 --> 00:19:54.290
to build our understanding

00:19:54.290 --> 00:19:56.400
and build our customer's understanding

00:19:56.400 --> 00:19:59.340
and finances understanding
of the market gap

00:19:59.340 --> 00:20:02.440
that exists that we don't
usually spot because it's a gap.

00:20:02.440 --> 00:20:04.320
We're not good and attuned at seeing gaps

00:20:04.320 --> 00:20:07.530
until after somebody comes
and puts a product into them.

00:20:07.530 --> 00:20:10.680
In aviation, we're dream
makers and dream deliverers,

00:20:10.680 --> 00:20:12.890
and you get it right and that's true,

00:20:12.890 --> 00:20:15.140
but you get the economics
wrong and it's not.

